chunks.representation:
Three equivalent readings of the theorem:

- $J = F\,\Delta t$ (impulse from a force and time);
- $J = \Delta p = m(v_f - v_i)$ (impulse from a momentum change);
- $F\,\Delta t = m(v_f - v_i)$ (the two set equal — solve for the unknown).

Work with signs, just as for momentum.

chunks.concept:
When an object must undergo a **fixed** change in momentum — a car crashing to a stop, a ball being caught, a person landing — the impulse $J = \Delta p$ is **set**. The force then depends entirely on the time:

$$F = \frac{\Delta p}{\Delta t}$$

**Longer stopping time → smaller force.** This is the physics behind:

- **crumple zones** and **airbags** — extend the time the occupant takes to stop;
- **bending your knees** when landing — lengthens the stop;
- **'giving' with a catch** (moving your hands back) — catches a ball over more time;
- **crash mats, packaging foam, boxing gloves** — all spread the impulse over more time.

The egg on concrete and the egg on a pillow undergo the **same** $\Delta p$; the concrete stops the egg ~100x faster, so it delivers ~100x the force — enough to break the shell.

A real collision force is not constant — it rises, peaks, and falls. For any force,

$$J = \text{area between the force-versus-time graph and the time axis}$$

For a constant force this area is a rectangle ($F\,\Delta t$); for a triangular pulse it is $\tfrac{1}{2}(\text{base})(\text{height})$; the **average force** is the height of the rectangle with the same area over the same time.

The theorem $F\,\Delta t = m(v_f - v_i)$ solves for whichever quantity is unknown:

- **impulse:** $J = m(v_f - v_i)$ or $J = F\,\Delta t$ (area);
- **force:** $F = J / \Delta t = m(v_f - v_i)/\Delta t$;
- **time:** $\Delta t = J / F$;
- **final velocity:** $v_f = v_i + J/m$.

Always carry signs, and state directions in the answer.
